**Verdict: MAJOR REVISION.**

The central negative conclusion survives: no fast classical constructor or unrestricted computational barrier has been established. Several headline interpretations do not survive, especially “exactly at capacity,” the minimum-weight equivalence, and the Cheng–Wan comparison.

I reproduced E1, E2, analysis, and all nine tests in scratch. All 800 records match the committed data after removing timing fields. P1–P5 hold, and both experimental tables check out. Additional counterexamples and the five-call construction below are reproducible with [audit.py](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/crtdecode_2/repro/audit.py).

| # | Severity | Location | Finding | Suggested fix |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **MAJOR** | §0; F2(b); §5.1 | **The mean-list calculation is correct; “exactly at capacity” is not established.** The coefficient \(\nu=2/(3\kappa)\) is where the leading exponent of the mean changes sign. At equality, unspecified lower-order terms can make the mean vanish or exceed every polynomial in \(n\). For example, take \(h=\rho(u_0)^{-1}e^{\pm g}\), where \(\log n\ll g\ll F_{2/3}\). Both choices have the same leading \(\nu\), but opposite list-size behavior. Moreover, averaging over the special centres \(-X\) in a dyadic interval is neither a channel-capacity theorem nor a worst-case list-decoding theorem. | Replace “code-specific capacity” with **leading-order average-list threshold**. Say the optimized parameters approach this boundary. To claim mean approximately one, specify \(h\) using the actual friable density, including lower-order terms. Remove “planted” unless a planting distribution is defined. |
| 2 | **MINOR** | F2(a),(c) | The uniform-alphabet calculation does give \(\nu=1/\kappa\): \(\log[V\binom nt y^{-t}]=(\nu-1/\kappa+o(1))F_{2/3}\). But this uses the surrogate normalization \(R=\log V/(n\log y)\), \(\alpha=t/n\), whereas F2(a) uses the compressed valuation normalization \(R=\log V/W\), \(\alpha=x/W\), with \(W\sim nx\). These are different metrics and benchmarks. “Textbook formulas misplace the threshold” misleadingly suggests those formulas apply to this code and fail. | Introduce fresh surrogate symbols and say **the uniform Hamming benchmark has a different threshold**. Retain the algebra; withdraw the implication that standard capacity theory predicts the wrong answer for its own model. |
| 3 | **MINOR** | F1; F2(a); F3 | F1’s gcd identity and fixed threshold are correct in the stated asymptotic regime. F2(a)’s rate and Johnson separation also check out. F3’s limits are correctly \(\kappa/\nu^2\) and \(3\kappa^2/\nu\), giving \(1/2\) and \(1\) at the specified optimum; \(V=n^{k(1+o(1))}\) follows. However, \(t=x/\log y\) is a **lower bound** on the number of distinct agreeing primes for a squarefree smooth integer, not an exact count for general smooth integers. A large power of two has only one prime coordinate contributing. | Keep the valuation formulation as the actual problem; label the squarefree dictionary a surrogate. State the coverage condition \(X+h\le3X_0\) explicitly when defining \(P\); it holds asymptotically but does not follow from \(h<X\) alone. |
| 4 | **MAJOR** | F4; main-results table | **Smooth does not mean minimum raw weight.** For \(X_0=X=8,h=2,y=5\), the prescribed \(P=720\). All of \(8,9,10\) are smooth, but their weights are \(\log(P/8),\log(P/9),\log(P/10)\); only \(10\) minimizes weight. With an empty smooth list, a minimum-weight word still exists. The interval translate is also not a group-theoretic affine coset. | State the valid pointwise equality: \(\operatorname{wt}(c(m))-(\log P-\log m)=\log(m/\gcd(m,P))=0\) iff \(m\) is smooth. On a nonempty promised window, minimizing raw weight produces the **largest** smooth integer, but finding any smooth integer is a weaker task. |
| 5 | **MAJOR** | Prop SG; its “Reading”; ladder | **SG does not cover weighted calls.** Let \(d=2^a\ge V\), and let \(P=d\prod_{3\le q\le y}q^{E_q}\), with adequate odd-prime exponents. Set \(w_2=1\), all other weights zero. Every multiple of \(d\) passes the weighted Johnson condition. Smooth multiples with odd cofactor divide \(P\), and their averaged yield has scale \(\rho(u_0)^{1-o(1)}\), much larger than \(VX^{-3/4+o(1)}\). Thus the claimed weighted extension of the count of certified \(z\mid P\) is false. Also, ordinary “full agreement” means \(P\mid z\), whereas SG studies \(z\mid P\). | Restrict SG to **fixed, unweighted, full-divisor certificates on the full window**. Delete the deduction that every Johnson-class success must use a cofactor test or implement the divisor trick. “Pure decoding” is an imposed restriction, not standard decoding terminology. |
| 6 | **MINOR** | SG proof | The restricted unweighted proof is sound: certification forces \(\log P\le(\log(3X_0))^2/\log V\); the prime-relabeling injection is valid; and the resulting smoothness bound gives exponent \(1/4\). Applying a theorem stated for fixed \(A\) to \(A(X)\to4/3\) needs justification, but monotonicity between fixed exponents \(4/3\pm\eta\) supplies it. The relevant friable estimate is standard. [Hildebrand–Tenenbaum, (1.14), Theorem 1.4](https://jtnb.centre-mersenne.org/item/10.5802/jtnb.101.pdf) | Add the fixed-exponent sandwich. Make fixedness of the modulus/support explicit wherever the bound is summarized. |
| 7 | **MINOR** | Prop DT | **Correctness and the advertised upper bounds survive.** Step 1 recovers low valuations; higher-power queries recover every remaining valuation. The leaf rule is exact because a nonsmooth cofactor is at least three while \(X+j<3\cdot2^{b-1}\). The worst-case and expected-depth bounds follow from the displayed union bound and geometric sum. But the displayed equality \(D=2n+1+\sum m_q\) is not universally exact: primes with \(a_q=0\) need no step-1 call, empty windows need no final call, and public exponent caps can avoid terminal queries. | Use \(D\le2n+1+\sum m_q\), stating when equality holds. Label this **PROVED in the unit-cost oracle model**. The empirical \(3n+1\) bound is a prediction that held, not a general consequence of DT. |
| 8 | **MINOR** | §0; §2.1; Lemma NU | NU is correct as a nonuniform comparison-tree construction. It does not establish that “every nonuniform depth measure” is \(O(x)\): permitted queries matter. Likewise, DT invalidates the proposed barrier only when complete oracle answers and arbitrary leaf computation are free. GC proves containment of certified sets, not a general statement about the computational value of searching those sets implicitly. | Replace “the natural adaptive model” with the actual model definition. Restrict NU’s conclusion to comparison trees with arbitrary precomputed constants. Distinguish set containment, query complexity, and runtime throughout. |
| 9 | **MINOR** | Prop PT; Props JC/AT; §4.1 interpretation | PT is valid for a deterministic pointer cap and an \(X\)-independent schedule. For nonsmooth \(d\), the yield is zero; for \(d>3X_0\), it is zero. Uniformly over the remaining range, the Dickman ratio is at most \(d^{\epsilon_X}\), \(\epsilon_X=O(\log(u_0\log u_0)/\log y)=o(1)\). This gives the claimed supremum bound and validates use of JC’s \(D>V\) case. However, this preserves only the leading \(L[2/3]\) exponent. It does not exclude substantial \(L[1/3]\) improvements. Conditioning on nonempty windows also requires division by their probability. | Include this uniform estimate directly. Retain the small-modulus and adaptive-pointer exclusions. Do not translate unconditional dyadic averages into promised-window guarantees without the conditioning factor. |
| 10 | **MAJOR** | §1.4–§1.5; §3.3, Cheng–Wan | The venues are correct: **FOCS 2004; SIAM J. Comput. 37(1), 195–209, 2007**. The claimed regime match is wrong. Their BDD reduction uses dimension \(k=g-\ell\) and \(g\ge(4/\varepsilon+2)(\ell+1)\), so agreement \(g=O(k)\). Here \(t/k\sim k/2\to\infty\). Their separate **list-decoding** reduction at a counting threshold cannot be substituted for hardness of finding one solution. The reduction also uses rational received centres, not simply the stated high-coefficient polynomial coset. [Journal statement](https://epubs.siam.org/doi/abs/10.1137/S0097539705447335), [reduction, §3](https://arxiv.org/pdf/math/0405082) | Remove “exactly this regime” and “Cheng–Wan’s BDD regime.” Present the split-polynomial connection as an analogy, distinguishing their listing theorem from their one-output theorem. |
| 11 | **MAJOR** | §1.5, CHHS/GSS rows | CHHS prove optimality for specified auxiliary-polynomial constructions for roots modulo the **full modulus**, at the \(N^{1/d}\) boundary. This is not a theorem proving the unknown-divisor/Johnson boundary optimal; their discussion explicitly identifies that extension with undeveloped joint-capacity questions. GSS’s published weighted CRT theorem uses relatively prime coordinates and equality scores; the nested valuation score is an additional model specialization. [CHHS, concluding discussion](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1605.08065), [GSS](https://people.csail.mit.edu/madhu/papers/2000/gss-conf.pdf) | Separate the published guarantees, the note’s idealized certificate class, and auxiliary-polynomial impossibility results. Do not cite CHHS as proving that the present certificate family stops at Johnson. |
| 12 | **MAJOR** | §3.2, subset-sum and generic-additive rows | “The size budget forces sparse supports \(\le u_0\)” reverses the basic inequality: factors bounded by \(y\) require **at least** \(x/\log y\) factors, counting multiplicity. Even distinct support can be larger: a primorial near \(X\) has about \(x/\log x\) primes, exceeding \(u_0\). Erickson’s \(\Omega(n^{\lceil r/2\rceil})\) theorem concerns arbitrary real inputs and \(r\)-sparse queries. It supplies no lower bound for fixed prime logarithms with a varying interval target; substituting growing \(r=u_0\) additionally requires tracking parameter dependence. KLM correctly supplies small comparison trees, including \(O(rn\log^2 n)\) for \(r\)-SUM. [Erickson](https://jeffe.cs.illinois.edu/pubs/pdf/linsat.pdf), [KLM, Theorems 1.1 and 1.3](https://people.csail.mit.edu/virgi/6.s078/papers/ksumldt.pdf) | Withdraw the transferred cost assertion. Restrict any balanced-factor model explicitly and justify its coverage. “Not applicable” is stronger than the arguments supplied for the subset-sum mechanisms. |
| 13 | **MINOR** | §1.5, 2026 RS papers | **The cited parameter obstruction checks out against both arXiv texts.** BCPZZ Theorem 4.1 requires \(k>\lceil\varepsilon^{-3/\theta}\rceil\), the rate condition, a small-\(\varepsilon\) condition, and prime \(q\ge\max(n,4\varepsilon^{1-9/\theta}n/k)\); runtime is \(q^{O(\varepsilon^{-12/\theta})}\). Direct substitution fails. Jeronimo Theorem 1.1 gives fixed additive slack and \(q^{O_\gamma(1)}\); here \(\gamma=(t-k)/n\to0\). His quantitative interpolation conditions likewise do not supply this regime. These are statement checks, not independent proof audits. [BCPZZ](https://arxiv.org/html/2609.08005v1), [Jeronimo](https://arxiv.org/html/2609.05870v1) | Replace “UNVERIFIED” with “theorem statement checked; proof not audited.” Include the field-size hypotheses and retain the missing CRT-transfer qualification. |
| 14 | **MINOR** | §1.3, cryptographic citations | Kiayias–Yung’s **ICALP 2002** citation is correct; it formulates a decisional assumption for randomly corrupted PR instances and derives cryptographic consequences. Naor–Pinkas’s original venue is **STOC 1999**. Bleichenbacher–Nguyen, **EUROCRYPT 2000**, analyze noisy interpolation with candidate-value lists and conditional lattice/SVP reductions, and discuss the integer analogue. This is not a general algorithm for coherent received words \(-X\). Naor–Pinkas’s revised full version distinguishes the broken repeated-abscissa assumption from its replacements. [Kiayias–Yung](https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2002/017/), [Naor–Pinkas](https://www.wisdom.weizmann.ac.il/~naor/PAPERS/ope.pdf), [Bleichenbacher–Nguyen](https://www.di.ens.fr/~pnguyen/pub_BlNg00.htm) | Supply titles and exact noise models. Keep these as related assumptions/attacks, not hardness transfers to the present distribution. |
| 15 | **NIT** | §1.4–§1.5, GV/GGG/BKR | These comparisons are substantially accurate. GGG’s parameters are \(K=N/2-d+1\), radius \(N-K-d\), \(d\le c\log N/\log\log N\), and field size \(2^{\mathrm{poly}(N)}\). GV proves NP-hardness for constructed RS maximum-likelihood instances. BKR constructs large lists using extension-field subspace structure. None proves the desired CRT one-output lower bound. [GGG, Theorem 1.1](https://arxiv.org/html/1611.03069v1), [GV](https://www.cs.cmu.edu/~venkatg/pubs/papers/mldrs.pdf), [BKR](https://sites.math.rutgers.edu/~sk1233/rsld.pdf) | Replace memory citations with these precise statements and preserve the existing non-transfer caveats. |
| 16 | **MINOR** | §1.4; §3.3; labels/novelty | The Frobenius identity and BGJT connection are correct, but **EUROCRYPT 2014 BGJT is heuristic**, relying on representation, smoothness, and rank assumptions. The \(L[1/3,1.232]\) DLI claim likewise inherits sihard’s heuristics; its unqualified appearance in §1.4 is too strong. F1’s formulation, NU, GC, PNP, and counting/search self-reduction are standard or elementary. “Ours, beyond codex” is not itself a literature novelty claim, but should not become one. [BGJT, introduction and §4](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1306.4244) | Mark inherited heuristic implications at each prominent occurrence. Reserve novelty language for the specialized quantitative statements and experiments. |
| 17 | **MINOR** | Prop PNP; Lemma SC | PNP is correct for the compact-input uniform problem: \(P=NP\) supplies polynomial-time witness search, contradicting any genuinely superpolynomial lower bound. SC’s exact-counting self-reduction also works, including a sentinel outcome for an empty interval. But “approximate counting does not help” overreaches. Relative approximations to two **cumulative** counts cannot resolve their tiny difference; a relative approximation directly to the interval count would help. Dyer’s FPRAS does not supply that thin-slab oracle. [Dyer](https://www.math.cmu.edu/~af1p/Teaching/MCC17/Papers/knapsack.pdf) | Narrow the approximation claim. State the trial-division cost separately from counting. Keep PNP as a standard complexity observation, not evidence for a particular hardness conjecture. |
| 18 | **MINOR** | E1/E2; preregistration; accounting | P1 ratios reproduce as **3.507, 3.761; 3.798, 3.947**. P2 reproduces as **4.390 and 5.484**; P3 holds. P4 is **100/100 per configuration**; P5 maxima are **231 and 400**. E2 mean depths are **220.08 and 386.76**, and every leaf-incidence count equals the sieve’s. No ground-truth leakage was found. Pointer/test accounting, budget checks, and PH’s integer threshold are correct. But P2’s hit groups also separate decoder policies, so this is a selected-test yield comparison, not an isolated causal effect of another hit. E1 excludes preprocessing, LCM bookkeeping, and sorting by design. | Retain all five scores and tables. Call costs **registered abstract costs**. Disclose the benign E2 change from the preregistered \(X+h\) exponent cap to a public bit-size cap; it enforces oracle-only access. |
| 19 | **MINOR** | `JohnsonOracle.call` | The generic oracle contract is false outside exercised calls. With \(X=15,M=30,\text{length}=2\), the certified pair \((0,15)\) is omitted because the general-modulus branch enumerates only full multiples of \(M\). **This does not invalidate E2:** prime-power calls are handled, and its final call contains \(M=z\), preventing another nearby point from meeting the Johnson threshold. The information decoder itself accesses \(X\) through answers and public size information. | Narrow the harness contract to supported calls, or implement complete enumeration. Replace the lexical “no leakage” test with interface-level checks if the harness is extended. |
| 20 | **MINOR** | §2.2 ladder; missed constructions | **A substantially smaller query tree is missing.** Query \(q^{\lfloor\log_q V\rfloor}\) for successive small primes, stopping when their product exceeds \(2^b\). The answers reconstruct \(X\) exactly by CRT. Free leaf sieving plus final certification takes \(O(x/\log h)+1=n^{o(1)}\) calls in this regime. On all 200 E2 windows, **four residue queries plus one certificate suffice**, versus 220–387 calls. This beats the divisor trick in the declared query model, while retaining the expensive leaf. | Add this simpler construction and strengthen the information-triviality statement. It supplies no charged-runtime breakthrough. |
| 21 | **MINOR** | §2.2–§3.2; computational scope | In a natural **quantum** model, BBHT/Grover search over offsets with reversible exact trial division costs \(n^{1+o(1)}\sqrt h=L[2/3,\nu/2]\) on promised windows. At \(\nu_*\), this halves the leading classical divisor-trick exponent—well beyond an \(L[2/3]^{o(1)}\) gain—although it still misses the requested \(L[1/3]\) target. [BBHT](https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9605034) For charged **classical** computation, a concrete omitted class is residue-guided beam search over subthreshold divisor products, retaining and combining information from failed candidates. PT/GC/AT do not rule it out; I have no justified asymptotic improvement for it. | State “classical” explicitly. Add subthreshold arithmetic search to the open ladder and cost every child probe, combination, restart, and verification before asserting any gain. |